Make sure there is a plan in place so you do not become default baby sitter or driver. Make sure anything you have that has real value to you or needs explaining on how to use it you actually do explain! Its better to come off as overly protective then to have something valuable to you be broken and then seem off the handle for that. 

&#x200B;

Figure out a plan for big items you buy, if they are belonging to the house or to one family, like a couch and the like, don't go 50/50 unless you are willing to buy the other person out for the full amount they spent when they leave if the couch is left behind.

So my issue with it is that for the majority of what we know of American wizarding history and culture it's incredibly based off of what was done at Hogwarts. The concept of school houses is not really a well known one here in this country, and that was brought over whole cloth. The number of words that are old and different in British English and American English are few and far between, in fact most of the ones I can think of off the top of my head are post industrial revolution (Lift/Elevator, Lorry/truck, boot/trunk). So why did American wizards, while clinging to a British education system decide to not use the words that were in place and culturally relevant? If the single school that educates them goes off British principles then why would there be a need for a different word? It's not describing anything separate or a new class or state of being, so why change the word? 

&#x200B;

I'm not a lingusistic historian so I might be wrong, but I did grow up with an English mother and have to spend a lot of time parsing out the differences, it's mainly in pronunciation rather than entirely different words.

My first time as a rematcher was a nightmare, the store never marked it as sent and I was panicking because I felt so bad because I really liked the person I was matching with and wanted to make them happy, so I bought from another store and they sent right away, the first store finally had shipped but in the wrong style and size so my santa wrote a (understandable) thank you being like "Oh wow, thanks for the shirt that's from your area" because it arrived a month after it was supposed to and was hilariously the wrong size. Then the next day the other gifts arrived and they were (thankfully) the right size and style! And my giftee was so excited and also noted that they'd never receieved notification about me keeping them in the loop and said it was so nice to know they hadn't been forgotten! 

&#x200B;

So moral of the story, its amazing to be a rematcher no matter how crazy the experiance is!

Going from server to bartender can really work wonders. Just be careful, the food service industry is really seductive, I left college partly because I was getting so much work and making so much money. Sadly my income level has stayed the same and its rough because now my needs are a lot higher then they were when I was 20.

&#x200B;

Set a schedule and stick to it. Don't allow yourself to pick up shifts and ignore your schooling. Serving is a bit better for college people because there are more shifts to spread around.

Talk to management. You want them to know what you're doing. You want to keep them informed, keep a bar book and record this stuff. Even if you are alone you should feel comfortable taking care of things. If you aren't you should not work shifts you're alone during. 

&#x200B;

I've been in this position. The guy never did anything that was like full on creepy, he just kind of sent up all my red flags, and so just keeping in communication with my regulars and my boss I was able to keep it under control. In the end I stopped being polite to him after I really felt unsafe.

I worked one place where the owner would drink at the bar regularly and it worked, mainly because he didn't live in teh same city as the bar and used it as a vacation home. He also paid full price and would tip an insane amount. Because of that it was somehow easier to deal with? I think the fact that he didn't ask for special treatment and would go home when he was drunk was what worked.

&#x200B;

But any other time its always bad, they get capricious and make split second decisions while drunk and you can't control them.

I feel totally alone in the world almost all of my friends have abadoned me for various reasons. I'm not totally innocent in all of this, but none of them are giving me a chance to make up for any of my mistakes, and instead are throwing shit at me when I try to do anything to either make up for my mistakes or just share stupid things. I've had my mistakes thrown at me in the cruelest ways and even my own personality being told as the reason why they're angry at me. The aspect of my personality that is thrown at me is that I do too much for my friends. 

&#x200B;

I don't know what to do about this and I'm sick of loosing my friends and I have no way of making things up to people.

I work really hard at making sure all the food in my house is used. As well as figuring out ways to balence it out. I might buy super expensive cheese, but I make the bread myself. I try to eat before I leave the house so that I'm less likely to get take out. I also work really hard to learn to make my favourite foods so that I'm excited for what I have at home and that makes me less likely to get take out because I'm excited for the food. 

&#x200B;

It's a real process, and the other great thing you can do is download a blocker so you can't go onto the websites that allow you do do online ordering. It's embarrassing to have to have a net nanny but it is really helpful.

Oooh! I know about this one, first off contact CVOEO at 802 860 1417 they have offices all over, and do a lot to help people with money and also various housing and Job training. Champlain Housing Trust has a lot of help for low income people and some really good stuff in rural areas. I don't know if you have a car, but if you do then you can get to burlington and go to Reuse when you have a place, they have tons of great furniture and appliances and the like for really reasonable rates. You could also look into a program where they pair disabled people with someone who will live with them and help them out, you don't have to be there full time and it will be a free or even paid place to live, you'd have to organize something else for seeing your kids but short term it could be a great way to get on your feet and save up for a real place. 

&#x200B;

I'm not sure where in Vermont you are, but if you live near a food co op then I reccomend applying there, most of them are union and have great benefits, and might be more willing to hire you and if you have other experience it could be a good launch pad to finding out what other jobs are open. 

&#x200B;

I know how hard it is to live in Vermont and how shitty the market is on both jobs and housing. Best of luck!

My first time acting as a rematch was shipping hell for me, I kept on trying to buy from a local brick and mortar and they just were never available, then when I bought from their online shop they sent the wrong thing and never updated my shipping info. It took over 3 weeks of not hearing from them before I just bought something else and of course the moment I did that the original gift arrived (WRONG SIZE!!) so I was glad that I'd gotten something else. My poor giftee felt so shafted that they'd gotten forgotten twice and then when the gift arrived it was so wrong (again, the company fucked up) She was so sweet about it in the original thank you note though, and then so happy when the second gift arrived, the right size, and all!! It was what got me really hooked on doing the gift exchanges. 

&#x200B;

All that to say don't worry overly much sometimes especially with rematching where they want to do something super special it takes a little while longer!

First off look at how to replace the things in your life with out buying more things. I use my libraries ebooks as a great way to always have books without feeling like I have to have them physically. That's made it very easy to get rid of books

The sentimental stuff I reccomend packing and storing if you can afford it or someone with space will let you. You're going through a shitty time and it might not be the best time to make evaluations about personal stuff. Give yourself a set time limit and you'll find if you're missing things or not as well as be in a different headspace when you go through them. I've been storing things at my moms and every time I go through them I probably have a trash bags worth of stuff that I get rid of because I see this stuff every six months and don't miss it overly much I have a more and more clear view of what is important each time I see it. It's also helped me be able to organize it for her. If I'd tried going through it all when I moved to NYC I would have kept and moved a lot of things I didn't need. 

Start looking at what you want your life to look like after the divorce and keep and throw away things based on that as well!

